  "global_context_pack": "# Global Context Pack: Predictive Framework for Membrane Fouling in Full-Scale MBRs\n\n## Core Thesis\nAn AI-driven predictive and interpretable framework combining feature engineering (robust scaling, moving average) and explainable AI (SHAP, feature importance) predicts membrane fouling dynamics in full-scale food wastewater MBRs, with CatBoost outperforming other models ($R^2 = 0.8374$) and identifying F/M ratio and MLSS as dominant fouling factors.\n\n## Output Language Policy\nEnglish. Wrap mathematical expressions, parameter symbols, metrics, and units in KaTeX formatting (e.g., $R^2$, $\\text{LMH/kPa}$, $\\text{mg/L}$).\n\n## Key Terminology and Acronyms\n- **MBR (Membrane Bioreactor)**: Biological wastewater treatment combined with microfiltration or ultrafiltration membrane separation.\n- **Membrane Fouling**: Deposition and adsorption of soluble microbial products, extracellular polymeric substances (EPS), colloids, and particulates on membrane pores and surfaces.\n- **TMP (Transmembrane Pressure)**: Pressure differential driving permeation across the membrane ($\\text{kPa}$).\n- **Spec. Flux (Specific Flux)**: Membrane permeability normalized as flux divided by TMP ($\\text{LMH/kPa} = \\text{L}/(\\text{m}^2\\cdot\\text{h}\\cdot\\text{kPa})$).\n- **F/M Ratio (Food-to-Microorganism Ratio)**: Operational parameter representing substrate loading rate per unit biomass ($\\text{kgCOD}/(\\text{kgMLSS}\\cdot\\text{d})$).\n- **MLSS (Mixed Liquor Suspended Solids)**: Concentration of suspended solids/biomass in the mixed liquor ($\\text{mg/L}$).\n- **SV30 (Sludge Volume after 30 min)**: Percentage settled sludge volume after 30 minutes (\\%).\n- **SVI (Sludge Volume Index)**: Settling characteristic of activated sludge (\\text{mL/g}).\n- **COD RM (COD Removal Efficiency)**: Percentage efficiency of chemical oxygen demand reduction (\\%).\n- **DO (Dissolved Oxygen)**: Aeration parameter affecting microbial activity and shear stress ($\\text{mg/L}$).\n- **HRT (Hydraulic Retention Time)**: Reactor retention duration (approximately $0.75\\text{ days}$).\n- **Robust Scaling**: Preprocessing method centering on median and scaling by interquartile range ($IQR = Q_3 - Q_1$).\n- **Moving Average (MA)**: Feature engineering window ($5\\text{-day}$ optimal) capturing cumulative temporal fouling trends.\n- **CatBoost**: Categorical Boosting decision tree ensemble algorithm utilizing symmetric trees and ordered boosting.\n- **SHAP (Shapley Additive exPlanations)**: Game-theoretic framework computing additive feature attributions ($\\phi_i$).\n\n## Section Overview\n- **Abstract**: Study rationale, methodology overview, primary findings ($R^2 = 0.8374$ for CatBoost), and dominant fouling drivers (F/M, MLSS).\n- **1. Introduction**: Limitations of conventional fouling models, advantages of machine learning and explainable AI in full-scale MBR operations, and research objectives.\n- **2. Materials and Methods**:\n  - 2.1: Full-scale food wastewater treatment plant profile (Beijing), operational monitoring, 194-day dataset, and 4 test case formulations.\n  - 2.2: Exploratory data analysis (EDA) including feature statistics, pair plot, scatter plot, Pearson correlation, and normality checks.\n  - 2.3: Data preprocessing and feature engineering via Robust Scaling and Moving Average transformations.\n  - 2.4: Model architectures: Statistical models (MLR, Ridge, Lasso) and Machine Learning models (SVR, RF, GBM, XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost).\n  - 2.5: Explainable AI methodologies: built-in and permutation feature importance, and SHAP game-theoretic attribution.\n- **3. Results**:\n  - 3.1: Data distribution, descriptive statistics, correlation matrices, and Shapiro-Wilk normality testing.\n  - 3.2: Implementation and impact of robust scaling and temporal moving average filtering.\n  - 3.3: Model performance evaluation across raw vs. engineered feature sets and four operational case formulations.\n  - 3.4: Final predictive performance validation of CatBoost against observed operational time-series.\n  - 3.5: Explainability analysis through built-in/permutation feature importance and SHAP summary plots, concluding with operational MBR control implications.\n- **4. Conclusions**: Summary of methodological contributions, field validation findings, practical decision-support value, limitations, and future work.\n"
